    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe request already carried `sharesHousehold` and `EnsureOwnerHouseholdUnique` already *allowed* a same-lastName/same-address joiner when it was set. The only missing piece was returning a stable, shared `householdId`. I implemented it as a **derived** field rather than a persisted one, which kept structural impact minimal \u2014 no DB schema, no `Owner` entity, and no pipeline changes.\n\nThree files touched:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` to the `Owner` response schema (alongside `customerCode`/`displayName`/`initials`). The generated `OwnerDto` picks up the field.\n\n2. **`HouseholdId.java`** (new, small, cohesive) \u2014 one static `of(Owner)` that hashes the normalized `lastName` + `address` (same case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed key `EnsureOwnerHouseholdUnique` uses to detect a clash) into a stable 16-char id.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one `@Mapping(... expression ...)` line delegating to `HouseholdId.of(owner)`, matching how `displayName`/`initials` are already computed.\n\n## Why derived works\n\nA household is exactly the set of owners sharing last name + address \u2014 the same key the clash check uses. Deriving the id from that key means every member of a household computes the *identical* value with no cross-owner write: owners `b` and `c` (same last name + address as `a`) get the same `householdId` automatically, satisfying \"assign both the same householdId\" and \"stable shared identifier\". Since `GET` re-maps the entity, the value is present on read without persistence.\n\nThe existing `EnsureOwnerHouseholdUnique` was left untouched \u2014 it already permits the joiner when `sharesHousehold` is true.",
